artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a Robertson–Walker braneworld cosmology obtained from a gravi-dilaton string effective action (or, equivalently, its Einstein-frame formulation), in which the matter content on the brane consists of a scalar field φ(t) with potential V(φ) coupled to a perfect fluid of energy density ρ and pressure p, with equation of state p = wρ. Assume the spatial sections are homogeneous and isotropic with scale factor a(t), and that the modified Friedmann equation on the brane has the schematic form H² = (8πG/3) ρ_tot (1 + ρ_tot/(2σ)) − k/a² + Λ/3, where σ is the brane tension and ρ_tot includes both the scalar-field energy density ρ_φ = ½φ̇² + V(φ) and the fluid energy density ρ. Suppose now that the scalar field and the fluid are allowed to exchange energy through a coupling term Q(φ,ρ,φ̇,H), so that the conservation equations read

  ρ̇_φ + 3H(ρ_φ + p_φ) = −Q,
  ρ̇ + 3H(ρ + p) = +Q.

Determine whether one can choose the potential V(φ), the equation-of-state parameter w, and the interaction term Q so that the resulting cosmological solution simultaneously:

 (i) is geodesically complete and free of curvature singularities for t → ±∞ (regular asymptotic behaviour at past and future infinity);

 (ii) satisfies the three constraints required of a physically admissible RW brane cosmology in the non-interacting case, namely:
   (a) the weak/strong/null energy conditions (or the appropriate brane energy condition) hold throughout the evolution;
   (b) the late-time evolution reproduces a standard cosmological epoch (e.g. an asymptotically de Sitter or radiation/matter-dominated phase consistent with observations);
   (c) the early-time behaviour avoids the initial Big Bang singularity, exhibiting either a bounce or an asymptotically flat past.

Either exhibit such an interacting model (V, w, Q) explicitly, or prove that no such choice exists.

